The first gift is your own alignment not unligned, and racially
appropriate.  Say you're a chaotic human so racially
appropriate doesn't apply and your class doesn't have a
guaranteed first gift.  You want to maximize the chance of
getting Stormbringer at the first gift so you get Sting and
Orcrist.  The chance of a gift remains 1-in-10 for sacs as
soon as your prayer timeout hits zero.  The chance of
getting Stormbringer or Grimtooth is 50-50 as that first gift.
Now you have gift_count=3D1 and artifact_count=3D3 so the
chance of getting a gift for the second artifact is
1/(10+2*(1*3)) or 1-in-16 and no matter which you got the
list of candidates for that second gift is then the remaining
chaotic one plus all of the unaligned ones.  Then the next
gift has odds of 1/(10+2*(2*5)) or 1-in-30.  Then 1/(10+2*(3*6))
or 1-in-46 then 1/(10+2*(4*7)) or 1-in-66 and so on.  For a
chaotic player who wants as many artifacts as possible
there's sense in getting Sting and Orcrist before starting a
sac cycle at an altar.  Eventually Sunsword and Demonbane
appear from Angles or Archons late in the game.  So as a
chaotic I'll get Sting and Orcrist before trying for my first
artifact, then if I get Grimtooth I'll try for Stormbringer as a
crowning gift.  But if I get Stormbringer as the first gift I'm
likely to try to avoid getting crowned.

Trying the same numbers for a lawful or neutral character
suggests that a player wants to avoid naming Sting or Orcrist
most of the time until having enough artifacts to give up any
sac cycle at an altar.

The main exception I've considered is wizards.  I am usually
willing to get Sting early on as practice towards Magicbane.
This is mostly because as a wizard I'm likely most interested
in Magicbane as a gift with maybe the effort needed to get
Stormbringer as a crowning gift when chaotic.

